1. What are program status registers ?
	
	Program status registers store status and control information about : 

		* Result of operations 
		* Current processor mode 
		* Interrupt enable/disable status
		* Instruction execution state (ARM/THUMB)

	ARM7 has 2 types of status registers

		1. CPSR :- Current Program status Register 
		2. SPSR :- Saved Program Status Register 

2. CPSR - Current Program Status Register 

	The CPSR reflects the current state of the processor 

	It contains : 

		* Condition flags 
		* Interrupt mask bits 
		* Execution state bit
		* Processor mode bits 
	
	CPSR is updated automatically by the processor during instruction execution

3. CPSR Bit layout (32 bit)

	The CPSR is a 32 bit register divided into fields

		[31:28] – Condition Flags

		[27:8] – Reserved / Control bits

		[7] – I bit (IRQ disable)

		[6] – F bit (FIQ disable)

		[5] – T bit (ARM/THUMB state)

		[4:0] – Mode bits

4. Condition Flags (N, Z, C, V)

	These flags are updated by data processing instructions when the S bit is set.

	N – Negative Flag

		Set if result is negative (MSB = 1)

	Z – Zero Flag

		Set if result is zero

	C – Carry Flag

		Set if carry occurs in addition

		Set if no borrow occurs in subtraction

	V – Overflow Flag

		Set if signed overflow occurs

	These flags are used by conditional execution. 

5. Interrupt Disable Bits
	
	I Bit – IRQ Disable

	I = 1 → IRQ disabled

	I = 0 → IRQ enabled

	F Bit – FIQ Disable

	F = 1 → FIQ disabled

	F = 0 → FIQ enabled

	These bits allow software to mask interrupts.

6. T Bit – Execution State Bit

	The T bit selects instruction execution state:

		T = 0 → ARM state (32-bit instructions)

		T = 1 → THUMB state (16-bit instructions)

	This bit changes when:

		BX instruction is executed

		Exception entry or return occurs

7. Mode Bits (M[4:0])

	The lowest 5 bits of CPSR define the processor mode.

	Common mode values:
		Mode		Binary	Purpose
		
		User		10000	Normal application mode
		FIQ		10001	Fast interrupt mode
		IRQ		10010	Interrupt mode
		Supervisor	10011	OS / reset mode
		Abort		10111	Memory fault handling
		Undefined	11011	Undefined instruction
		System		11111	Privileged user mode

8. SPSR – Saved Program Status Register

	The SPSR is used during exception handling.

		* Each exception mode has its own SPSR

		* SPSR stores the previous CPSR value

	This allows the processor to restore state after exception handling.

9. When SPSR Is Used

	During an exception:

		1. Current CPSR is copied to SPSR

		2. Processor switches to exception mode

		3. CPSR is updated for exception handling

	On exception return:

		SPSR is copied back to CPSR

10. Relationship Between CPSR and SPSR
	
	CPSR				SPSR

	Holds current processor state	Holds previous state
	Always present			Only in exception modes
	Modified during execution	Used only for restoration

	User and System modes do not have SPSR.

11. Accessing CPSR and SPSR

	ARM provides special instructions to access status registers:

		* MRS – Move PSR to register

		* MSR – Move register to PSR

	These instructions allow controlled modification of CPSR fields.

12. Importance of CPSR and SPSR

	Enables conditional execution

	* Controls interrupts

	* Manages processor modes

	* Enables ARM/THUMB switching

	* Essential for exception handling

13. Summary

	* CPSR holds current processor status

	* SPSR saves CPSR during exceptions

	* N, Z, C, V flags control conditional execution

	* I and F bits control interrupts

	* T bit selects ARM or THUMB state

	* Mode bits define processor mode 
 
